 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Activation-Informed Coarse-to-Fine Rendering](12277-activation-informed-coarse-to-fine-rendering.md)
-This method accelerates inference by conditionally skipping the expensive fine-pass MLP evaluation based on intermediate activation values from the coarse pass [12277-activation-informed-coarse-to-fine-rendering.md]. It differs from standard coarse-to-fine rendering by using a conditional mask derived from network internals (activations) rather than fixed density thresholds or sampling strategies. It is chosen when inference speed is prioritized over the marginal quality gain of the fine pass for background rays.
+A method to accelerate NeRF inference by skipping the expensive fine-pass MLP evaluation for rays determined to be empty or background, based on intermediate activation values from the coarse pass [12277-activation-informed-coarse-to-fine-rendering]. It distinguishes itself from siblings like [Last Layer Re-training](13532-last-layer-re-training.md) by operating during the forward pass to prune computation rather than optimizing the model structure post-training [12277-activation-informed-coarse-to-fine-rendering].
 
 ### [Last Layer Re-training](13532-last-layer-re-training.md)
-Last Layer Re-training propagates a color edit from a single view to a pre-trained NeRF by selectively retraining only the last layer of the color MLP [13532-last-layer-re-training.md]. It differs from full re-training by preserving the geometric and view-dependent information encoded in deeper layers, enabling near real-time editing. It is chosen for interactive editing tasks where global consistency must be maintained with minimal computational overhead.
+A technique for instantly propagating a color edit from a single view to an entire pre-trained Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) by selectively retraining only the last layer of the color Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) [13532-last-layer-re-training]. It solves the problem of efficient 3D scene editing by avoiding the computational cost and visual artifacts associated with full model retraining, while preserving existing view-dependent information such as specularities [13532-last-layer-re-training]. Unlike [Activation-Informed Coarse-to-Fine Rendering](12277-activation-informed-coarse-to-fine-rendering.md), which focuses on rendering efficiency during optimization, this method focuses on post-training parameter modification for editing [13532-last-layer-re-training].
 
-### Rip-NeRF Optimization
-Rip-NeRF optimization involves learning the tiny MLP weights and the ripmaps $R$. Model weights are initialized following Glorot and Bengio (2010), while ripmaps $R$ start from a uniform distribution over $[-0.01, 0.01]$ [S1]. The optimizer is AdamW [S1]. The base learning rate is $2 \times 10^{-3}$, scaled by 10x for $R$ due to its direct scene representation role [S1]. The learning rate schedule reduces the rate by 0.6x at steps 60K, 90K, 100K, and 108K, over a total of 120K iterations [S1]. The batch size is dynamically adjusted to maintain approximately 256K spheres per batch [S1].
